Beryllium: no GPU memory buffers for video frames on FreeBSD with NVIDIA

Purpose: on the owner's machine (FreeBSD 16.0-CURRENT, NVIDIA RTX 5090
  Laptop, driver 595.99.02, KDE Wayland) every video crashed its tab with
  "Error code 11" (SIGSEGV in the renderer) as soon as playback began:
  H.264, VP9 and AV1, plain files and Media Source Extensions alike, on
  YouTube and on local test files. Audio-only playback worked. The stock
  FreeBSD Chromium 156.0.8078.47 crashed the same way, so the fault is in
  the shared FreeBSD/NVIDIA path, not in Helium or Beryllium.
  freebsd/tests/browser/run videodiag --diagnose (3 November 2026, both
  browsers) replayed the clips under switches that each turn one part of
  the video path off. Only these avoided the crash: no GPU at all,
  SwiftShader, X11 instead of Wayland, headless, and
  --disable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames. Hardware decode, VA-API and
  decode threads made no difference. So the renderer faults while writing
  decoded frames into GPU memory buffers (GBM buffer objects that the
  NVIDIA driver allocates and the renderer maps; the imported BSD patch
  patch-media_gfx_mappable__shared__image__video__frame__pool.cc lets
  FreeBSD use that path as Linux does).
  On FreeBSD with an NVIDIA GL driver the renderer now copies frames the
  ordinary way (shared memory, uploaded by the GPU process), as it does
  with --disable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames. The driver is recognised
  by the GL vendor string, because FreeBSD reports PCI vendor 0x0000 in
  the GPU information (beryllium://gpu, "GPU0: VENDOR= 0x0000").
  --enable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames turns the faster path back on,
  for testing a driver that fixes the fault. Other drivers are unchanged.
  The same flag also governs camera (media stream) frames, which take the
  same path.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on the imported BSD patch
  patch-content_renderer_render__thread__impl.cc (stage 2).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench.
Upstream: the crash affects the FreeBSD www/chromium port too; to be
  reported there with the videodiag table and backtraces.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/run media (H.264, VP9, AV1 clips play; MSE
  plays) and run videodiag --diagnose ("as normally run" plays).
security-review: not required (turns a code path off; no new input
  handling)

--- a/content/renderer/render_thread_impl.cc
+++ b/content/renderer/render_thread_impl.cc
@@ -1015,13 +1015,29 @@
            .status_values[gpu::GPU_FEATURE_TYPE_ACCELERATED_VIDEO_ENCODE] ==
        gpu::kGpuFeatureStatusEnabled);
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
+  bool enable_gpu_memory_buffers =
+#else
   const bool enable_gpu_memory_buffers =
+#endif
       !is_gpu_compositing_disabled_ &&
 #if !BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
       !cmd_line->HasSwitch(switches::kDisableGpuMemoryBufferVideoFrames);
 #else
       cmd_line->HasSwitch(switches::kEnableGpuMemoryBufferVideoFrames);
 #endif
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
+  // Beryllium: with NVIDIA's FreeBSD driver, the renderer dies with SIGSEGV
+  // as soon as it writes a decoded video frame into a GPU memory buffer
+  // (every codec; the stock FreeBSD Chromium too). Copy frames the ordinary
+  // way there; --enable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames opts back in. The GL
+  // vendor names the driver: FreeBSD reports PCI vendor 0x0000 here.
+  if (gpu_channel_host->gpu_info().gl_vendor.find("NVIDIA") !=
+          std::string::npos &&
+      !cmd_line->HasSwitch(switches::kEnableGpuMemoryBufferVideoFrames)) {
+    enable_gpu_memory_buffers = false;
+  }
+#endif
   const bool enable_media_stream_gpu_memory_buffers = enable_gpu_memory_buffers;
   bool enable_video_gpu_memory_buffers = enable_gpu_memory_buffers;
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
